<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Evening Routine</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Brush My Teeth.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Explain keywords from readings in simpler terms. Concept: Explain simple events in story form.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain keywords about bedtime routines using gestures (e.g., mimes sleeping for "sleep"). Can explain simple bedtime events in story form with basic actions (e.g., "First, I brush my teeth." and act it out). Can guide peers in understanding bedtime routines by pointing to visuals (e.g., points to bed and says, "Sleep here.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards of evening routine items (e.g., toothbrush, pajamas, bed, book). The teacher asks, "What is this?" and "What do you do?" to prompt students to recall vocabulary from previous lessons. Students respond chorally or individually. The teacher encourages miming actions like "brush teeth" or "sleep."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models describing a very simple evening routine using sequencing words and actions. "First, I wash my face. Then, I brush my teeth. Last, I go to bed." The teacher emphasizes the pronunciation of /w-ɒ-ʃ/ in "wash" and /t-iː-θ/ in "teeth" while performing the actions. The teacher also models saying, "Goodnight, sleep well!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and sequencing words.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I brush my teeth before I eat dinner, or after I eat dinner?" (After)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I am sleepy, do I want to play or sleep?" (Sleep)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher mimes yawning) "What am I doing? Am I tired or clean?" (Tired)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Evening Routine".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, illustrated sequential story chart or a short, simple picture book depicting a child's evening routine (e.g., "Timmy's Bedtime"). The text accompanying each picture will be simple sentences using sequencing words and target vocabulary, such as: "First, Timmy eats dinner. Then, he washes his face. Next, he brushes his teeth. Last, he reads a story and goes to bed."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will be able to understand the sequence of simple events in a story and identify key actions and objects related to an evening routine.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to pictures. "What is the story about? Morning or night?" (Night). "What does Timmy do before he sleeps?" (Brushes teeth, reads story).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Ordering Pictures:</b> The teacher presents mixed-up picture cards from the story. Students help the teacher put them in the correct sequence on the board.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Explaining simple events using sequencing words (First, then, next, last) and present simple tense for daily routines. Target vocabulary: bed, pajamas, teeth, toothbrush, brush, wash, wear, sleep, read, story, sleepy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Sentence Completion (Individual Work):</b> The teacher provides sentence frames on a worksheet with pictures, focusing on "First, then, next, last." Students complete the sentences using provided vocabulary words. E.g., "First, I ___ (brush) my ___ (teeth). Then, I ___ (wear) my ___ (pajamas)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Guided Explanation with Visuals (Choral & Individual):</b> The teacher shows a series of large routine flashcards one by one. For each card, students chorally say the action, e.g., "Brush teeth!" The teacher then prompts: "What do you do *first*?" "First, I brush my teeth." "What do you do *next*?" "Next, I put on my pajamas." The teacher specifically encourages explicit explanations of actions through miming.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Explanation (Pair Work - Mediation Focus):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 4-5 picture cards depicting different evening routine steps (e.g., eat dinner, brush teeth, wear pajamas, read book, sleep). Student A arranges the cards in a sequence and describes their routine using "First, then, next, last." Student B's role is to *explain* back what Student A is doing, using the same sequencing words, and *mediating* understanding by pointing to the visuals and using gestures. If Student A says "I am sleepy," Student B can point to the picture of sleeping and mime yawning to explain "sleepy."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Evening Routine".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group presentation and explanation of their "Bedtime Routine Chart" (21st Century Skills Project).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are divided into small groups (3-4 students). Each student presents their individual "Bedtime Routine Chart" (created in previous lessons or as homework). The focus is on *mediation*: explaining their chart to their group members.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student takes turns showing their chart. They explain each step of their routine, pointing to the pictures and using sequencing words ("First, I..., Then, I..., Next, I..., Last, I...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Crucially, other group members act as the audience. If they don't understand a word (e.g., "pajamas" or "blanket"), the presenter must *explain* it in simpler terms or using gestures (e.g., mime pulling a blanket, point to their clothes and say, "sleep clothes").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students should also practice bedtime politeness by asking questions like, "Can you read me a bedtime story, please?" when relevant to their chart, and responding, "Goodnight, sleep well!" to their peers at the end of their routine explanation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing, providing support, and encouraging students to use their charts as a visual aid for their explanations.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students explain their "Bedtime Routine Chart" to their small group, actively using mediation strategies (gestures, pointing, simple explanations) to ensure their peers understand their routine steps.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can explain keywords (e.g., "sleepy," "toothbrush") using gestures or simple words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can explain simple bedtime events in story form (using "First, then, next, last").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Guides peers in understanding by pointing to visuals on the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary (bed, pajamas, teeth, brush, read, sleep) and sequencing words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of /b/ in "bed" and /t/ in "teeth" is generally intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates polite expressions (e.g., "Goodnight!").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a simple exit ticket.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">I can explain my bedtime routine to a friend: (Draw a happy, neutral, or sad face).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One new word I can explain to my friend is ____________. (e.g., sleepy, pajamas)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy to explain about your routine using pictures and words?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult to explain, and how did you try to make your friends understand?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This lesson focused on mediation, allowing students to consolidate their learning by explaining their "Bedtime Routine Charts." Most students were able to use sequencing words and target vocabulary, especially with visual support. The use of gestures to explain keywords was effective for many at the A1.1 level. Some students needed more prompting to simplify language for their peers. I noticed good engagement during the group presentations, and the self-assessment helped gauge individual understanding. For future mediation tasks, I might provide more sentence starters for simplifying explanations (e.g., "It means...", "It's like..."). The blend of TPR and visual aids was crucial.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "My Evening Routine" theme. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly "My Morning Routine," building on similar vocabulary and grammatical structures, with a new set of 21st Century Skills Project activities.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>